  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1..High pressure has shifted offshore, with\nsoutherly outherly onshore flow beginning in earnest today. \nAtmospheric profile to gradually moisten today, although we\nshould still be too dry aloft for any convection to form along\nthe sea breeze. While rain chances are still below 15% today,\nthere should be more cloud cover along and ahead of the sea\nbreeze coinciding with peak heating. Otherwise seasonable temps\nforecast today as highs reach the upper 80s to near 90s inland,\nand mid 80s along the coast. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...ENC once again enters into a period of \nunsettled weather starting on Sunday. An upper trough will be \ndeepening over the Ohio Valley Sun morning as it moves towards \nthe Eastern Seaboard, while at the surface an associated closed \nlow is expected to traverse the Great Lakes region. As these \nfeatures move east, they will encounter the Bermuda high which \nis expected to stall this upper trough, surface low, and its \nassociated fronts before they reach the Eastern Seaboard. While \nthe upper trough stalls, embedded shortwaves rounding the base \nof the trough are forecast to advect into the Carolinas Sun \nthrough at least Mon/Tue. These features, in addition to the sea\nbreeze each afternoon/evening, will be the main forcing present\nfor a notable increase in precip chances. \n\nThere will be two areas of focus for shower and thunderstorm \nactivity on Sunday. The first will be Sun morning along and behind a \nN'ward moving warm front/coastal trough with much of the activity \noccuring near and east of Hwy 17. We should still be too dry\naloft inland as the boundary moves through, so thunder chances\nremain low inland Sunday morning. Proximity to the gulf stream\ncould allow isolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms to \nreach coastal communities south of Oregon Inlet. The second will\nbe in the afternoon/evening with shower and thunderstorm \nactivity coming in from the west associated with the stalled \nupper trough. Cloud cover will play a big role in how high \ninstability can truly get, but with the dewpoints rising there \nshould be enough instability entering the region for 1000-2000 \nJ/kg of MLCAPE Sunday. While shear is not too impressive with \nthe unidirectional SE flow, the combination of long, skinny \nCAPEs, PWATs over 2\", and slow storm motions could result in \nheavy rainfall rates and wet microbursts where storms develop. \nThe best coverage is expected to be to our west closer to I-95, \nwith more isolated to scattered coverage hinted at by CAMs along\nour coastal plain. As a result, have trended down a tad with \nPoPs Sunday PM with this update. Further reductions to PoPs may \nbe necessitated with future updates if this trend holds. \n\nMonday the trough is still stalled to our west and gradually\nweakens. This reduces the potential for height falls over the\nregion despite the trough creeping a bit closer. Still, we\nshould see some weak embedded shortwaves moving through aloft. \nSlightly better forcing, paired with another day of long skinny\nCAPEs and PWATs north of 2\" brings higher precip chances \ncompared to Sunday with heavy rainfall rates and a low end wet \nmicroburst threat and slow storm motion vectors.  All this \nbrings flash flooding concerns to mind, especially with how much\nrain has been observed in many locations in the past 1-2 weeks.\nWe are currently in a Marginal (level 1/5) Excessive Rain \nOutlook for Sunday. What prevents it from being higher is likely\nthe uncertainty on instability with antecedent cloud cover and \nthe lack of substantial ground-level forcing. With high temps \nforecast to only be in the low to mid 80s Monday, the sea \nbreeze may be weak in nature. \n\nBeyond Monday, the trough/low is still expected to linger to our \nwest until eventually it gets picked up and shifted to our north by \nanother trough moving through the Great Lakes region. As a result, \ndaily elevated precip chances continue through mid-week with a low \nend risk for flash flooding.",
